# ── Docker build context ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# An allowlist, not a denylist. The builder stage does `COPY . .`, so every path
# that reaches this context is both shipped into a layer and part of that
# layer's cache key — which means a denylist has two failure modes that both bit
# this repo:
#
#   • Cache. Editing a test file or adding a screenshot changed `COPY . .` and
#     invalidated `next build` behind it. The slowest stage in the image was
#     being rebuilt from scratch by changes it cannot even observe.
#   • Data. `data/` and `.data/` hold case stores, audit logs and scan reports.
#     They were landing in the builder layer and, with `cache-to: mode=max`,
#     in the exported build cache. Nothing published them, but nothing needed
#     them either.
#
# Listing what to keep fails safe: something new and irrelevant is excluded by
# default, and a genuinely required file announces itself by breaking the build
# immediately rather than by quietly bloating every image.
#
# `*` matches one path segment, so it excludes top-level entries only; anything
# under a re-included directory was never excluded and needs no rule of its own.

# Exclude everything at the root…
*

# …then re-admit exactly what `next build` reads.
!package.json
!package-lock.json
!next.config.mjs
!tsconfig.json
!next-env.d.ts
!postcss.config.mjs
!tailwind.config.ts
!src
!public

# Belt and braces for things that can appear *inside* the allowed trees.
**/.DS_Store
**/*.local
**/node_modules
